<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmed Sékou Touré</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sékou Touré</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmed Sékou Touré was a Guinean political leader and the first president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. In 1958 he led Guinea to reject the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> in a referendum, making it the first French colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a> to choose immediate independence. His stand is a notable episode in the decline of French colonial governance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmed-sekou-toure.jpg" alt="Ahmed Sékou Touré" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Knudsen. White House Photographs. John F. Kennedy Presidential Library and Museum, Boston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmed_Seko%C3%BA_in_1962_at_White_House_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>De Gaulle's French Community of 1958 offered autonomy within a French framework; Guinea under <b>Sékou Touré</b> rejected it outright and took immediate independence, and within two years the remaining African territories had followed, dissolving the entire administrative edifice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the referendum of 28 September 1958 on Charles de Gaulle’s French Community, Guinea under <b>Ahmed Sékou Touré</b> voted no and became independent on 2 October 1958; the other territories voted yes and became autonomous republics within the Community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Nyerere of Tanzania, <b>Sékou Touré</b> of Guinea, Modibo Keïta of Mali, Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt, Haile Selassie of Ethiopia and Kenneth Kaunda of Zambia shaped the OAU.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ahmed Sekou Toure</b> and his Democratic Party of Guinea campaigned against the 1958 constitution, and on 28 September 1958 Guinea voted no. It became independent on 2 October 1958, and France withdrew its staff and aid abruptly.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-guinea">French Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
